---
// The home page, as the mockup (site/mockup.html) has it. Its examples come from the docs and its
// numbers from the README, at build time, so the page says what the repo does at this commit.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CodeCard from '../components/CodeCard.astro';
import PerfChart from '../components/PerfChart.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import { docsIndex } from '../docs-index.mjs';
import { lineHtml } from '../markdown.mjs';
import { errorOf, fences } from '../examples.mjs';
import { buildTime, tickLoop } from '../numbers.mjs';

// parallel.md: its first example, the C that example compiles to (the page's first two C blocks),
// and the example that must fail to compile.
const parallel = fences('reference/parallel.md');
const calx = parallel.filter((f) => f.lang === 'calx');
const c = parallel.filter((f) => f.lang === 'c');
const example = calx[0].code;
const exampleC = `${c[0].code}\n\n// and in main, where the loop was:\n${c[1].code}`;
const failing = calx.map((f) => errorOf(f.code)).find((e) => e !== null);
if (!failing) throw new Error('docs/reference/parallel.md has no example marked // error:');

// What stash reads and answers: the first two text blocks of the guide's first page.
const [reads, answers] = fences('guide/getting-started.md').filter((f) => f.lang === 'text');
const guide = docsIndex().find((s) => s.id === 'guide')!;

const bench = tickLoop();
const windows = bench.platforms[0];
// Times on Windows, the first column.
const check = buildTime('calx check');
const size = check.label.match(/(\d+) modules \(([\d,]+) lines\)/);
if (!size) throw new Error(`README.md's "${check.label}" row doesn't say how many modules and lines`);
const rebuild = buildTime('Debug rebuild').windows;
const unchanged = buildTime('calx run of it again').windows;
---

<Base
	title="Calx: fast, safe code that gives the same answer every time"
	description="Calx is a general-purpose compiled language: memory-safe without a garbage collector, with math defined to the bit on every machine, and parallel loops that give the same result on any number of cores."
>
	<main class="home">
		<div class="hero-art">
			<canvas class="crystal" data-scene="hero" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
			<svg class="hero-logo" viewBox="0 12 399.761 92" fill="currentColor" role="img" aria-label="Calx">
				<use href="#logo-shapes"></use>
			</svg>
		</div>

		<div class="hero-copy">
			<span class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span>Pre-release · build it from source</span>
			<h1 class="display">Fast, safe code that gives the same answer every time</h1>
			<p class="lede">
				Calx is a general-purpose compiled language. It's memory-safe without a garbage collector, its math is defined
				to the bit on every machine, and its parallel loops give the same result on one core or twenty-four. On our
				benchmark it takes {bench.ratio}× the time Rust does.
			</p>
			<div class="cta">
				<a class="btn primary" href="/guide/getting-started/">
					Learn Calx <svg class="i" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-arrow"></use></svg>
				</a>
				<a class="btn" href="/docs/#reference">Read the reference</a>
			</div>
			<div class="fineprint">Compiles to C · Windows and Linux · MIT licensed</div>
		</div>

		<div class="stats">
			<div class="stat">
				<div class="v">{bench.ratio}×</div>
				<div class="l">Rust's time per tick, with the same<br />algorithms and the same checksum</div>
			</div>
			<div class="stat">
				<div class="v">{unchanged}</div>
				<div class="l"><code>calx run</code> on a program<br />that hasn't changed</div>
			</div>
			<div class="stat">
				<div class="v">0</div>
				<div class="l">warnings: everything the checker<br />objects to is an error</div>
			</div>
		</div>

		<section class="section">
			<div class="split">
				<div>
					<div class="kicker">Parallel loops</div>
					<h2 class="h">Every core, and the same answer every time</h2>
					<p class="intro">
						<code>parallel for</code> runs a loop's iterations across a pool of threads. The checker makes sure no iteration
						touches what another one changes, so the program prints the same thing on one thread or twenty-four.
					</p>
					<ul class="facts">
						<li>
							<svg class="i" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-check"></use></svg>
							<span>The check is the ownership check, applied between iterations. It costs nothing at run time.</span>
						</li>
						<li>
							<svg class="i" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-check"></use></svg>
							<span>What iterations print comes out in their order, and a panic reports the first iteration that panicked.</span>
						</li>
						<li>
							<svg class="i" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-check"></use></svg>
							<span>
								Calx compiles to C. The C tab shows what this loop becomes: a function per chunk, its pointers marked
								<code>restrict</code>.
							</span>
						</li>
					</ul>
					<a class="more" href="/reference/parallel/">Read about parallel loops</a>
				</div>
				<div class="stack">
					<CodeCard calx={example} c={exampleC} file="game.calx" />
					<div class="note">Reach too far, and it won't compile:</div>
					<pre class="term"><span class="path">main.calx:{failing.line}:{failing.column}:</span> <span class="e">error:</span> <span class="msg">{failing.message}…</span>
{'  ' + failing.text}
{'  ' + ' '.repeat(failing.column - 1)}<span class="caret">{'^' + '~'.repeat(failing.token.length - 1)}</span></pre>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="section">
			<div class="kicker">Why Calx</div>
			<h2 class="h">Six promises, and where to check each one</h2>
			<p class="intro">
				Every claim on this page links to the page that defines it, and the examples on those pages are compiled and run
				on every push.
			</p>
			<div class="claims">
				<div class="claim">
					<div class="ic"><svg class="i" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-bolt"></use></svg></div>
					<h3>Faster than Rust</h3>
					<p>
						On the tick-loop benchmark, which uses the same algorithms and gives the same checksum as its Rust reference,
						Calx takes about {bench.ratio}× Rust's time, with one thread or 24.
					</p>
					<div class="proof"><b>{windows.calx1.toFixed(2)}</b> vs {windows.rust1.toFixed(2)} ms per tick</div>
					<a class="more" href="#perf">The benchmark</a>
				</div>
				<div class="claim">
					<div class="ic"><svg class="i" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-eq"></use></svg></div>
					<h3>Deterministic math</h3>
					<p>
						Integers wrap, divide and shift, and floats compute and print, the same way on every platform. The rules match
						Java's, so code ported from Java gives the same bits.
					</p>
					<div class="proof"><code>-16 &gt;&gt;&gt; 28</code> is <b>15</b>, as in Java</div>
					<a class="more" href="/reference/integers/">Integers and floats</a>
				</div>
				<div class="claim">
					<div class="ic"><svg class="i" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-shield"></use></svg></div>
					<h3>Memory-safe by default</h3>
					<p>
						Ownership is checked while the program compiles and costs nothing while it runs: no garbage collector, no
						reference counts. Unchecked code is opt-in, inside <code>unsafe</code>.
					</p>
					<div class="proof">0 GC pauses, by construction</div>
					<a class="more" href="/reference/ownership/">Ownership</a>
				</div>
				<div class="claim">
					<div class="ic"><svg class="i" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-par"></use></svg></div>
					<h3>Deterministic parallelism</h3>
					<p>
						<code>parallel for</code> runs a loop on every core, and the compiler checks that no iteration touches what another
						changes. Same output with one thread or many.
					</p>
					<div class="proof"><b>{windows.calx24.toFixed(2)}</b> ms per tick on 24 threads</div>
					<a class="more" href="/reference/parallel/">Parallel loops</a>
				</div>
				<div class="claim">
					<div class="ic"><svg class="i" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-strict"></use></svg></div>
					<h3>Strict by default</h3>
					<p>
						No implicit conversions, no dropped values, no unused names and no shadowing. Each mistake is an error that says
						what to write instead.
					</p>
					<div class="proof">every rule is an error, not a warning</div>
					<a class="more" href="/reference/strictness/">Strictness</a>
				</div>
				<div class="claim">
					<div class="ic"><svg class="i" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-clock"></use></svg></div>
					<h3>Fast to build</h3>
					<p>
						<code>calx check</code> reads {size[1]} modules, {size[2]} lines, in {check.windows}. A small program runs in about
						0.3 s from an empty cache, and in under 25 ms when nothing changed.
					</p>
					<div class="proof"><b>{rebuild.replace(/ ms$/, '')}</b> ms to rebuild after one change</div>
					<a class="more" href="/reference/command/">The calx command</a>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="section" id="perf">
			<div class="kicker">Performance</div>
			<h2 class="h">Measured on every push</h2>
			<p class="intro">
				The tick-loop benchmark is a synthetic simulation, a world of packed block storage, entity physics in doubles, hash
				maps and messages between regions, stepped one tick at a time. It runs the same algorithms in Calx and in Rust and
				checks that both give the same checksum. CI runs it on every push, on Windows and Linux, and the raw results are in
				the repo.
			</p>
			<PerfChart />
		</section>

		<section class="section">
			<div class="split">
				<div>
					<div class="kicker">Learn</div>
					<h2 class="h">Learn it by building one program</h2>
					<p class="intro">
						The guide builds <code>stash</code>, a small inventory program: it reads commands, keeps count of what's put away
						and taken out, and says what went wrong when a command doesn't make sense. Each page adds to it.
					</p>
					<ol class="steps">
						{
							guide.pages.map((p) => (
								<li>
									<a href={`/${p.slug}/`}>
										<div>
											<b>{p.title}</b>
											<span set:html={lineHtml(p.about, { links: false })} />
										</div>
									</a>
								</li>
							))
						}
					</ol>
				</div>
				<div class="io">
					<div><div class="cap">It reads</div><pre>{reads.code}</pre></div>
					<div><div class="cap">It answers</div><pre>{answers.code}</pre></div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<div class="band">
			<canvas class="crystal" data-scene="band" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
			<div class="inner">
				<div class="text">
					<div class="kicker">Built with Calx</div>
					<h2 class="h">Calcite, a Minecraft server written in Calx</h2>
					<p>
						Calcite is a Minecraft Java Edition server that matches vanilla bit for bit while running faster. It's the largest
						Calx program so far: deterministic math keeps its worlds the same as vanilla's, parallel loops tick its regions on
						every core, and its plugins are written in Calx too.
					</p>
					<div class="pills"><span>Bit-exact worldgen</span><span>Parallel regions</span><span>Plugins in Calx</span></div>
					<p class="link"><a class="more" href="https://github.com/ellipog/calcite">Calcite on GitHub</a></p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>

		<SiteFooter />
	</main>
</Base>

<script>
	import '../scripts/crystal.js';
</script>
